Flink SQL filters on TIMESTAMP(9) can discard matching rows because timestamp literals are truncated to microseconds. Preserve higher-precision literals as timestamp strings so filter binding and source split serialization retain all digits.
The SQL example to reproduce this problem:
SET 'execution.runtime-mode' = 'batch';
SET 'table.dml-sync' = 'true';

CREATE TABLE trades (
    trade_id INT,
    trade_time TIMESTAMP(9)
) WITH (
    'format-version' = '3',
    'write.format.default' = 'parquet'
);

INSERT INTO trades
VALUES (
    1,
    CAST('2024-01-01 09:30:00.123456789' AS TIMESTAMP(9))
);

-- Verify that the trade exists.
SELECT trade_id, trade_time FROM trades;

-- Look up that exact trade timestamp.
SELECT trade_id
FROM trades
WHERE trade_time =
    CAST('2024-01-01 09:30:00.123456789' AS TIMESTAMP(9));

-- Before the fix:  the query will return no rows.
-- After the fix:  the query will return tradeI_id = 1.
